var is a keyword used to declare a variable.
Think of a variable as a labeled storage box. When you type var token, you are telling the browser: "Create a storage box, label it 'token', and save this specific value inside it so I can use it later.

FormData as a digital envelope designed to mimic an actual HTML form filled out by a user. Instead of sending raw text, it structures data into clean key-value pairs (like postId: 8 or comment: [stolen cookies]) 
so the web server can read it exactly like a normal form submission.

fetch is a tool in javascript:

It transmits the contents of var data to a specified endpoint.
'/post/comment' (The Endpoint):
This is the destination URL where the data is being sent.
no-cors', the browser is told to send a "simple" request and ignore the response.

The window.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', ...) command is an event listener. In JavaScript, its job is to wait until the webpage's structure is completely loaded before 
running the code inside it

Without the listener: If the script runs the exact millisecond the page starts loading, the browser won't have processed the HTML form elements yet. 
The document.getElementsByName('csrf')[0] element won't exist in the browser's memory, causing the script to crash with a TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined error.
With the listener: By wrapping everything in DOMContentLoaded, the script guarantees that the HTML input field named csrf is present in the browser's DOM. 
This ensures that it successfully grabs the anti-CSRF token and the user's document.cookie before using fetch to send them out.


